---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'Operations Overview', href: '/docs/operations' },
  { title: 'Two-Host', href: '/docs/operations/two-host' },
  { title: 'Single-Host', href: '/docs/operations/single-host' },
  { title: 'Hardening', href: '/docs/operations/hardening', active: true },
  { title: 'Runbooks', href: '/docs/operations/runbooks' },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'network', text: 'Network Hardening', level: 2 },
  { id: 'auth', text: 'Authentication & Access', level: 2 },
  { id: 'lua', text: 'Lua Pipeline Hardening', level: 2 },
  { id: 'logging', text: 'Logging & Monitoring', level: 2 },
  { id: 'runtime', text: 'Runtime Hardening', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Production Hardening" description="Security hardening recommendations for production deployment" section="Operations" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    Hardening here has two goals that pull in opposite directions: make the honeypot look real enough to
    engage an attacker, and make sure a compromised honeypot cannot become a path into production or the
    SIEM. The pool credential split is the non-negotiable part.
  </p>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <DocCard title="Isolate the pools" icon="box" tag="Critical" description="Every pool database user must have a different password. The flat container network means network segmentation alone does not isolate pools." meta="test_pool_isolation.sh" />
    <DocCard title="Gate diagnostics" icon="lock" tag="Critical" description="Leave INTERNAL_TEST_SECRET unset in production, or require X-Internal-Test-Auth before emitting route and score headers." meta="fail closed" />
    <DocCard title="Sign the session" icon="key" tag="Important" description="Set SESSION_SIGNING_KEY explicitly. An unset key is generated per process and breaks cookie verification across restarts." meta="HMAC" />
    <DocCard title="Limit exposure" icon="shield" tag="Important" description="Firewall the SIEM and admin surfaces, keep the edge as the only public entry point and restrict backend access to trusted proxies." meta="least privilege" />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="network">Network Hardening</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Place honeypot behind a TLS-terminating reverse proxy with valid certificates.</li>
    <li>Restrict inbound access to the honeypot backend to trusted proxies only.</li>
    <li>Enable firewall rules limiting SIEM/Kibana access to authorized hosts.</li>
    <li>Isolate the honeypot network segment from production backend networks.</li>
    <li>Mind the layer's blast radius: the reverse-proxy layer gives each session a <a href="/docs/engine/layers">separate decoy backend</a> that absorbs file-upload and RCE attacks, whereas the database layer swaps only the database — a foothold there reaches the shared eshop filesystem, so prefer the reverse-proxy layer on an exposed deployment.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="auth">Authentication & Access Control</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Use strong, unique admin credentials for WordPress and SIEM interfaces.</li>
    <li>Enable multi-factor authentication where supported.</li>
    <li>Rotate API keys and credentials for Kibana/Elasticsearch.</li>
    <li>Apply least-privilege access for dashboard operators and analysts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="lua">Lua Pipeline Hardening</h2>
  <h3>IP Whitelisting</h3>
  <p><code>_G.config.threat.ip_whitelist</code> contains CIDR ranges or exact IPs that receive a negative reputation score (-20) and are never diverted to the honeypot. Should include: operator's admin IP, Tailscale tunnel range (100.64.0.0/10), university/lab network (10.0.0.0/8), loopback (127.0.0.1/32, ::1/128).</p>
  <h3>Session Cookie Security</h3>
  <p>The session cookie is named <code>SERVERID</code> (mimics HAProxy stickiness cookie). Signed with HMAC key (<code>SESSION_SIGNING_KEY</code>). When the key comes from env var, a bad signature is scored as tampering (+50). When unset, a random key is generated at startup.</p>
  <h3>Threat Decay Configuration</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><code>THREAT_DECAY_ENABLED=false</code> turns decay OFF entirely — scores persist indefinitely.</li>
    <li><code>DECAY_OFFENSE_SLOWDOWN</code> (default 0.75): each offense multiplies half-life by (1 + this).</li>
    <li><code>PERMAFLAG_OFFENSES</code> (default 5): at/above this many offenses, score stops decaying.</li>
    <li><code>PERMAFLAG_SCORE</code> (default 100): a single request scoring this also permaflags.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>AbuseIPDB Configuration</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><code>ABUSEIPDB_API_KEY</code> — when nil/placeholder, client disables itself.</li>
    <li><code>ABUSEIPDB_CONFIDENCE_MINIMUM</code> (default 90) — bulk feed confidence threshold.</li>
    <li><code>ABUSEIPDB_DAILY_CHECK_LIMIT</code> (default 1000) — free-tier daily cap.</li>
    <li><code>ABUSEIPDB_BLACKLIST_ENABLED</code> (default false) — bulk blacklist pull opt-in.</li>
    <li><code>ABUSEIPDB_BLACKLIST_REFRESH_HOURS</code> (default 24) — minimum age before fresh call.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Internal Test Secret</h3>
  <p><code>INTERNAL_TEST_SECRET</code> gates the X-Route-Target / X-Risk-Score response headers. When set, headers only emitted for requests with matching X-Internal-Test-Auth header. When unset, headers never emitted (fail closed).</p>
  <h3>Device-Signature Key</h3>
  <p>The RSA private key that decrypts the consent-gated <code>gdpr_token</code> must live only on the proxy and
  never be shipped to the SIEM or logged — the privacy guarantee is that only the one-way
  <a href="/docs/detection/signature">signature</a> ever leaves the server. Keep <code>GDPR_TIMESTAMP_WINDOW</code>
  tight to limit token replay, and treat the key like any other production secret.</p>

  <h2 id="logging">Logging & Monitoring</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ship structured logs to the SIEM with appropriate retention.</li>
    <li>Forward Suricata alerts to the SIEM in real time.</li>
    <li>Enable audit logging for administrative actions.</li>
    <li>Monitor <code>_G.utils.log_security_event()</code> output in Nginx error logs via Vector.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="runtime">Runtime Hardening</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Keep WordPress, plugins, and themes updated.</li>
    <li>Disable XML-RPC amplification vectors where feasible.</li>
    <li>Rate-limit login and REST API endpoints.</li>
    <li>Monitor file upload directories for suspicious activity.</li>
    <li>Regularly review threat_intel shared dict for poisoned entries.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
